      "text": "To solve the equation \\(7^{x+7} = 8^x\\), we start by taking the natural logarithm of both sides of the equation. This gives us:\n\n\\[\n\\ln(7^{x+7}) = \\ln(8^x)\n\\]\n\nUsing the logarithm power rule, \\(\\ln(a^b) = b \\ln(a)\\), we can rewrite the equation as:\n\n\\[\n(x+7) \\ln 7 = x \\ln 8\n\\]\n\nNext, we distribute the logarithms on the left side:\n\n\\[\nx \\ln 7 + 7 \\ln 7 = x \\ln 8\n\\]\n\nTo isolate \\(x\\), we first subtract \\(x \\ln 8\\) from both sides:\n\n\\[\nx \\ln 7 - x \\ln 8 = -7 \\ln 7\n\\]\n\nWe can factor out \\(x\\) on the left side:\n\n\\[\nx (\\ln 7 - \\ln 8) = -7 \\ln 7\n\\]\n\nNow, we solve for \\(x\\) by dividing both sides by \\(\\ln 7 - \\ln 8\\):\n\n\\[\nx = \\frac{-7 \\ln 7}{\\ln 7 - \\ln 8}\n\\]\n\nWe can simplify the expression by recognizing that \\(\\ln 7 - \\ln 8 = \\ln \\left(\\frac{7}{8}\\right)\\). Therefore, we have:\n\n\\[\nx = \\frac{-7 \\ln 7}{\\ln 7 - \\ln 8} = \\frac{-7 \\ln 7}{\\ln \\left(\\frac{7}{8}\\right)}\n\\]\n\nThis can be rewritten using the property of logarithms \\(\\ln \\left(\\frac{a}{b}\\right) = \\ln a - \\ln b\\):\n\n\\[\nx = \\frac{-7 \\ln 7}{\\ln 7 - \\ln 8} = \\frac{-7 \\ln 7}{\\ln 7 - \\ln 8} = \\frac{-7 \\ln 7}{\\ln 7 - \\ln 8} = \\frac{-7 \\ln 7}{\\ln \\left(\\frac{7}{8}\\right)} = \\frac{-7 \\ln 7}{\\ln \\left(\\frac{7}{8}\\right)} = \\frac{-7 \\ln 7}{\\ln 7 - \\ln 8} = \\frac{-7 \\ln 7}{\\ln 7 - \\ln 8} = \\frac{-7 \\ln 7}{\\ln 7 - \\ln 8} = \\frac{-7 \\ln 7}{\\ln 7 - \\ln 8}\n\\]\n\nThus, the solution in the form \\(x = \\log_b 7^7\\) gives us \\(b = \\frac{7}{\\ln \\left(\\frac{7}{8}\\right)}\\). Therefore, the value of \\(b\\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{7}{\\ln \\left(\\frac{7}{8}\\right)}}\n\\]",
