    {
      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To solve the equation \\(7^{x+7} = 8^x\\), we start by expressing both sides with the same base if possible. However, since 7 and 8 are different bases, we will take the natural logarithm (or any logarithm) of both sides to bring them into a simpler form.\n\nStarting with the given equation:\n\\[7^{x+7} = 8^x\\]\n\nTaking the natural logarithm of both sides, we get:\n\\[\\ln(7^{x+7}) = \\ln(8^x)\\]\n\nUsing the logarithm power rule \\(\\ln(a^b) = b \\ln(a)\\), we can rewrite the equation as:\n\\[(x+7) \\ln(7) = x \\ln(8)\\]\n\nNow, we expand and rearrange the terms to isolate \\(x\\):\n\\[x \\ln(7) + 7 \\ln(7) = x \\ln(8)\\]\n\\[x \\ln(7) - x \\ln(8) = -7 \\ln(7)\\]\n\\[x (\\ln(7) - \\ln(8)) = -7 \\ln(7)\\]\n\\[x = \\frac{-7 \\ln(7)}{\\ln(7) - \\ln(8)}\\]\n\nWe need to express \\(x\\) in the form \\(x = \\log_b 7^7\\). This means we need to find \\(b\\) such that:\n\\[x = \\log_b 7^7\\]\n\nBy the definition of logarithms, \\(\\log_b 7^7 = \\frac{\\ln(7^7)}{\\ln(b)} = \\frac{7 \\ln(7)}{\\ln(b)}\\). Therefore, we need:\n\\[\\frac{7 \\ln(7)}{\\ln(b)} = \\frac{-7 \\ln(7)}{\\ln(7) - \\ln(8)}\\]\n\nBy canceling \\(\\ln(7)\\) from both sides, we get:\n\\[\\frac{7}{\\ln(b)} = \\frac{-7}{\\ln(7) - \\ln(8)}\\]\n\\[7 (\\ln(7) - \\ln(8)) = -7 \\ln(b)\\]\n\\[\\ln(7) - \\ln(8) = -\\ln(b)\\]\n\\[\\ln\\left(\\frac{7}{8}\\right) = \\ln(b)\\]\n\\[b = \\frac{7}{8}\\]\n\nThus, the value of \\(b\\) is:\n\\[\\boxed{\\frac{7}{8}}\\]",
