      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To solve the equation \\(7^{x+7} = 8^x\\) for \\(x\\), we start by expressing both sides of the equation with the same base. However, since 7 and 8 are not powers of each other, we will use logarithms to solve it.\n\nFirst, take the natural logarithm (or any logarithm) of both sides of the equation:\n\\[\n\\ln(7^{x+7}) = \\ln(8^x)\n\\]\nUsing the logarithm power rule \\(\\ln(a^b) = b \\ln(a)\\), we can rewrite the equation as:\n\\[\n(x+7) \\ln 7 = x \\ln 8\n\\]\nNext, we expand and rearrange the equation to isolate \\(x\\):\n\\[\nx \\ln 7 + 7 \\ln 7 = x \\ln 8\n\\]\n\\[\nx \\ln 7 - x \\ln 8 = -7 \\ln 7\n\\]\nFactor out \\(x\\) on the left side:\n\\[\nx (\\ln 7 - \\ln 8) = -7 \\ln 7\n\\]\nSolve for \\(x\\) by dividing both sides by \\(\\ln 7 - \\ln 8\\):\n\\[\nx = \\frac{-7 \\ln 7}{\\ln 7 - \\ln 8}\n\\]\nWe can simplify the denominator using the property of logarithms \\(\\ln a - \\ln b = \\ln \\left(\\frac{a}{b}\\right)\\):\n\\[\nx = \\frac{-7 \\ln 7}{\\ln \\left(\\frac{7}{8}\\right)}\n\\]\nThus, the solution \\(x\\) can be expressed in the form \\(x = \\log_b 7^7\\). To find \\(b\\), we need to express \\(\\log_b 7^7\\) in terms of a logarithm with base 10. Recall that \\(\\log_b a = \\frac{\\ln a}{\\ln b}\\), so:\n\\[\nx = \\log_{\\frac{7}{8}} 7^7\n\\]\nTherefore, \\(b = \\frac{7}{8}\\). The final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{7}{8}}\n\\]",
